WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.066 --> 00:00:04.826
Bryan Singer's neo-noir classic The Usual
Suspects follows the interrogation of seemingly

00:00:04.835 --> 00:00:10.075
helpless Roger "Verbal" Kint, one of only
two survivors of a devastating fire on a ship

00:00:10.075 --> 00:00:11.886
docked at the Port of Los Angeles.

00:00:12.376 --> 00:00:17.786
Kevin Spacey's character spins the detectives
and viewers a long and twisted tale of drugs,

00:00:17.805 --> 00:00:22.426
deceit and death, explaining that everything
comes back to a mysterious mob boss by the

00:00:22.426 --> 00:00:23.986
name of Keyser Söze.

00:00:24.366 --> 00:00:25.026
The twist?

00:00:25.386 --> 00:00:30.726
Kint was Keyser Söze all along, which the
officers realize only a few moments too late.

00:00:31.546 --> 00:00:36.325
As Kint exits the police station and shuffles
off down the street, his awkward gait starts

00:00:36.326 --> 00:00:39.526
to straighten out - and we realize we've been
had.

00:00:40.286 --> 00:00:44.746
Quentin Tarantino's gleefully inaccurate take
on the World War Two thriller was nominated

00:00:44.755 --> 00:00:49.456
for eight Academy Awards, with Christoph Waltz
taking home the Oscar for his portrayal of

00:00:49.456 --> 00:00:52.006
the film's unforgettable villain: Hans Landa.

00:00:52.766 --> 00:00:57.266
Inglourious Basterds follows Lieutenant Aldo
"The Apache" Raine and his Basterds as they

00:00:57.276 --> 00:01:02.777
go on the hunt for Nazi scalps, finally machine-gunning
Hitler himself - although Landa is the the

00:01:02.786 --> 00:01:04.886
one German who Raine cannot kill.

00:01:05.106 --> 00:01:08.646
“Ooh, that’s a bingo!

00:01:11.586 --> 00:01:13.286
Is that the way you say it, ‘that’s a
bingo?’”

00:01:13.626 --> 00:01:14.626
“You just say bingo.”

00:01:14.786 --> 00:01:18.936
After striking a deal with the top brass,
the notorious villain is afforded his freedom

00:01:18.936 --> 00:01:22.526
in the States, though Aldo doesn't let him
slide that easily.

00:01:23.126 --> 00:01:27.386
With the film all but wrapped up, Lt. Raine
pulls out his knife and proceeds to carve

00:01:27.386 --> 00:01:31.126
a Swastika into Landa's forehead in a shockingly
gory close-up.

00:01:32.166 --> 00:01:38.026
Loosely based on French author Pierre Boulle's
1963 novel La Planète des Singes, 1968's

00:01:38.026 --> 00:01:42.765
Planet of the Apes was included in Empire's
Greatest Movies of All Time list and contains

00:01:42.766 --> 00:01:45.446
one of the most iconic examples of a twist
ending.

00:01:45.686 --> 00:01:50.766
Astronaut George Taylor, played by Charlton
Heston, leads a group of astronauts stranded

00:01:50.766 --> 00:01:55.326
on a strange and seemingly deserted planet
after their spaceship crash lands there - but

00:01:55.686 --> 00:01:58.726
the crew soon come to realize that they are
not alone.

00:01:59.026 --> 00:02:03.206
Apes with human-like intelligence dominate
this world, and they don't take kindly to

00:02:03.216 --> 00:02:07.705
the arrival of their otherworldly guests,
though it ultimately turns out Taylor and

00:02:07.706 --> 00:02:09.346
his team are far from alien.

00:02:10.066 --> 00:02:14.996
As he escapes the apes and makes his way along
the shoreline, he comes across the sandy remains

00:02:14.996 --> 00:02:19.736
of the Statue of Liberty - and discovers he's
been on Earth the whole time, trapped in a

00:02:19.736 --> 00:02:21.846
future devastated by nuclear war.

00:02:22.746 --> 00:02:26.646
Orphan follows a couple trying to move on
with their lives after the death of their

00:02:26.646 --> 00:02:27.546
unborn child.

00:02:28.155 --> 00:02:31.705
In an attempt to save their marriage, the
pair decide to go ahead with their plans for

00:02:31.706 --> 00:02:35.716
a third child, adopting a quiet 9-year-old
Russian girl named Esther.

00:02:35.986 --> 00:02:38.506
At least, they're lead to believe she's nine.

00:02:38.666 --> 00:02:43.986
In truth, Esther is a 33-year-old woman named
Leena Klammer, a psychopath with a growth

00:02:43.996 --> 00:02:49.345
disorder who's spent the majority of her life
posing as a pigtailed child and being repeatedly

00:02:49.346 --> 00:02:49.846
adopted.

00:02:50.466 --> 00:02:52.426
Why does she need to keep finding new families?

00:02:52.786 --> 00:02:55.606
Because she keeps trying to get it on with
the dads.

00:02:55.886 --> 00:03:01.126
Audiences watch in sheer horror as Esther
sets about seducing her new father - and finally

00:03:01.126 --> 00:03:02.446
kills him when he refuses her.

00:03:03.246 --> 00:03:07.386
The first Star Wars sequel picked up three
years after the destruction of the Death Star.

00:03:07.606 --> 00:03:12.196
Luke Skywalker and the Rebel assault squadron
bearing down on the super-weapon's weak spot

00:03:12.196 --> 00:03:16.446
remains a memorable enough finale for that
first film, but it pales in comparison to

00:03:16.446 --> 00:03:18.946
the closing stages of The Empire Strikes Back.

00:03:19.606 --> 00:03:24.226
Han Solo being frozen alive in carbonite and
carted off was a shock for fans of the dashing

00:03:24.235 --> 00:03:29.166
pilot, though the real jaw-dropper comes when
Luke arrives at Cloud City, spurred on by

00:03:29.166 --> 00:03:31.106
a premonition of Han and Leia in pain.

00:03:31.986 --> 00:03:36.366
The young Jedi falls right into Vader's trap,
losing his hand during the lightsaber battle

00:03:36.366 --> 00:03:37.246
that ensues.

00:03:37.856 --> 00:03:41.726
In a moment that would be quoted, referenced
and parodied countless times over the years

00:03:41.726 --> 00:03:45.906
that followed, Vader makes cinema’s most
notorious parental declaration.

00:03:46.046 --> 00:03:49.806
“No, I am your father.”

00:03:49.866 --> 00:03:54.566
David Fincher's gory thriller Seven is crammed
full of shocking moments, but one scene in

00:03:54.566 --> 00:03:58.006
particular sticks long in the memory: the
film's haunting finale.

00:03:58.486 --> 00:04:03.026
After veteran detective William Somerset and
his new partner David Mills finally manage

00:04:03.036 --> 00:04:07.746
to corner the killer, they discover a box
containing the head of Mills' pregnant wife

00:04:07.746 --> 00:04:08.326
Tracy.

00:04:09.096 --> 00:04:13.746
Despite it being exactly what the killer wants,
the heartbroken detective guns down the skin-headed

00:04:13.746 --> 00:04:19.096
madman in a heartbreaking climax that, unlike
many of the movie’s other scenes, doesn’t

00:04:19.096 --> 00:04:21.106
need to rely on gore to be shocking.

00:04:21.866 --> 00:04:27.886
A loose remake of 2002 Hong Kong crime thriller
Infernal Affairs, Martin Scorsese's Best Picture

00:04:27.896 --> 00:04:33.116
winner The Departed moves the action out of
Asia and brings it to Boston, home of notorious

00:04:33.116 --> 00:04:38.346
Irish mob boss Whitey Bulger - the inspiration
behind Jack Nicholson's character, Frank Costello.

00:04:39.006 --> 00:04:43.366
The veteran gangster relies on his police
mole Colin Sullivan to stay ahead of the game,

00:04:43.746 --> 00:04:47.846
though he doesn't realize he also has a double
agent in his ranks: Billy Costigan.

00:04:48.706 --> 00:04:53.766
But after finally exposing Sullivan, Costigan
is unceremoniously shot in the head by another

00:04:53.766 --> 00:04:55.126
of Costello's moles.

00:04:55.376 --> 00:05:00.286
Even more unexpected to audiences, however,
was the double-blow which followed: Staff

00:05:00.286 --> 00:05:04.686
Sergeant Dignam showing up right at the final
moment to put a bullet in Sullivan's temple.

00:05:05.406 --> 00:05:10.106
Another Scorsese-DiCaprio collaboration with
an unexpected ending, psychological thriller

00:05:10.106 --> 00:05:14.566
Shutter Island follows U.S. Marshal Teddy
Daniels and his new partner Chuck.

00:05:15.126 --> 00:05:19.466
They visit Ashecliffe Hospital for the Criminally
Insane to investigate the disappearance of

00:05:19.466 --> 00:05:22.426
a woman incarcerated there for drowning her
children.

00:05:22.846 --> 00:05:27.386
Scorsese delivers a gripping mystery that
leads viewers down a winding path with a stunning

00:05:27.386 --> 00:05:27.886
end.

00:05:28.426 --> 00:05:32.786
Just as Teddy believes he's about to solve
the puzzle of the missing woman, he discovers

00:05:32.786 --> 00:05:37.846
that his real name is Andrew Laeddis and he's
actually a patient at the hospital, sent there

00:05:37.846 --> 00:05:41.426
for killing his wife - who had previously
killed their children.

00:05:41.906 --> 00:05:45.706
The whole investigation was actually a ruse
by the head of the facility, designed as a

00:05:45.706 --> 00:05:49.406
last-ditch effort to snap Laeddis out of his
conspiracy-obsessed insanity.

00:05:50.446 --> 00:05:54.806
Based on the life and travels of American
hiker Christopher McCandless, Sean Penn's

00:05:54.816 --> 00:06:00.376
Into The Wild is a biographical survival film
that spends 148 minutes building up to a happy

00:06:00.376 --> 00:06:02.186
ending that never comes.

00:06:02.396 --> 00:06:06.686
The audience gets to know McCandless as he
lives in complete isolation in the Alaskan

00:06:06.686 --> 00:06:10.646
wilderness, intercut with scenes depicting
the long road he took to get there.

00:06:11.186 --> 00:06:15.786
As the film draws to an end, McCandless begins
to realize that true happiness can only be

00:06:15.786 --> 00:06:20.266
shared with others, and plans to return home
to his family - but the river he crossed four

00:06:20.266 --> 00:06:22.306
months earlier has become impassible.

00:06:23.086 --> 00:06:27.986
On the point of starvation, the young backpacker
is forced to gather roots and plants to eat,

00:06:27.986 --> 00:06:31.266
accidentally consuming a poisonous one in
the process.

00:06:31.526 --> 00:06:35.206
The film ends with McCandless crawling into
his sleeping bag to die.

00:06:36.066 --> 00:06:40.526
Writer/director Stephen Chbosky wasn't just
aiming to make another teen-centric indie

00:06:40.526 --> 00:06:44.666
flick when he decided to adapt his own novel,
The Perks of Being a Wallflower, for the big

00:06:44.666 --> 00:06:45.166
screen.

00:06:45.846 --> 00:06:49.966
He also wanted to make his story, which tackled
mental health issues among young people, a

00:06:49.966 --> 00:06:54.446
more "communal experience" - in the hope of
reaching more of those affected by its themes.

00:06:54.886 --> 00:06:58.946
The film itself is very well acted by its
leads, though it's hard to put your finger

00:06:58.946 --> 00:07:03.106
on what was so good about their performances
when all you can think about is the ending.

00:07:03.696 --> 00:07:07.326
By the end of the movie, troubled protagonist
Charlie has been through the ringer and is

00:07:07.326 --> 00:07:12.056
about to finally get the girl - but when she
touches his leg, he has a flashback of his

00:07:12.056 --> 00:07:13.026
late Aunt Helen.

00:07:13.696 --> 00:07:18.086
The film comes to a grim close with Charlie
admitting to a psychiatrist that he's been

00:07:18.086 --> 00:07:21.726
repressing memories of the sexual abuse he
suffered from his Aunt.

00:07:22.346 --> 00:07:27.266
Frank Darabont's adaptation of the Stephen
King novella The Mist caused controversy before

00:07:27.266 --> 00:07:32.106
it was even released, after the director chose
to go with a different ending from the book.

00:07:32.106 --> 00:07:36.306
King fans were put at ease when the author
gave his approval of the changes, though only

00:07:36.306 --> 00:07:40.326
a man who has spent a lifetime delving into
the bleak could ever claim to have enjoyed

00:07:40.326 --> 00:07:40.826
it.

00:07:41.246 --> 00:07:45.646
The film follows a group of people trapped
in a supermarket by a deadly mist full of

00:07:45.646 --> 00:07:46.966
tentacled creatures.

00:07:47.456 --> 00:07:51.786
In the end, protagonist David and his young
son manage to escape in a car, though when

00:07:51.796 --> 00:07:56.106
they run out of gas David is forced to use
his remaining four bullets on his child and

00:07:56.106 --> 00:07:57.026
passengers.

00:07:57.306 --> 00:08:01.406
After shooting them all, he steps out of the
car to accept his gruesome fate at the hands

00:08:01.406 --> 00:08:05.426
of the monsters, only for the mist to clear
and the U.S. Army to appear.

00:08:06.266 --> 00:08:10.626
If there's one director known for his mastery
of the twist ending, it's M. Night Shyamalan

00:08:10.866 --> 00:08:15.326
- a man who enjoys nothing more than lulling
his audience into a false sense of security,

00:08:15.326 --> 00:08:17.966
only to flip the world he's created on its
head.

00:08:18.466 --> 00:08:24.006
He did it to great effect in 2000's Unbreakable
and 2004's The Village, but his most famous

00:08:24.006 --> 00:08:28.226
shock ending came in his first major success:
The Sixth Sense.

00:08:32.326 --> 00:08:33.806
“I see dead people.”

00:08:34.426 --> 00:08:38.735
The story follows a kid named Cole as he tries
to talk through his ability to see ghosts

00:08:38.735 --> 00:08:40.746
with a troubled child psychologist.

00:08:40.986 --> 00:08:44.826
Unless you've been living under a rock, of
course, you’ll know that Willis' character

00:08:44.836 --> 00:08:49.346
was actually dead the whole time - and when
the film first came out, news of its shocking

00:08:49.346 --> 00:08:51.336
finale spread like wildfire.

00:08:51.546 --> 00:08:56.126
Arguably, it was the power of that twist which
helped this small-scale supernatural horror

00:08:56.126 --> 00:09:03.266
bag six Oscar nominations and a staggering
worldwide box office total of $673 million.

00:09:08.579 --> 00:09:13.559
brand of suspense earned him a well-deserved
reputation as “the Master of Suspense”

00:09:14.159 --> 00:09:17.979
- something he delivered consistently over
a distinguished, five-decade career.

00:09:18.559 --> 00:09:22.889
Perhaps his most famous shock ending is the
one he conceived for Psycho, the story of

00:09:22.889 --> 00:09:24.939
Norman Bates and his family's motel.

00:09:25.659 --> 00:09:30.319
While Janet Leigh being slashed in the shower
is undoubtedly the movie's best-known scene,

00:09:30.319 --> 00:09:35.329
the most shocking moment of all comes in the
closing stage; we finally realize Norman's

00:09:35.329 --> 00:09:40.139
mother has been dead all along and he's been
committing murders dressed in her clothing.

00:09:40.359 --> 00:09:44.639
Hitchcock went to great lengths to keep the
twist a secret, swearing the cast and crew

00:09:44.639 --> 00:09:50.299
to an oath of secrecy and imploring the public
to keep quiet about Bates via the film's advertisements.

